xi→ yj exists, we know that {u,v} is an edge of G, so we conclude from the deﬁnition of
xi→yj that each arca0,...,a i−1 is intersected by the arcbj, and also by each of bj+1,...,b t′
because of the ordering of the arcs. This contradicts the fact that h is a homomorphism.
Conversely, assume that the VDCS formula is satisﬁed. Then for each vertex of u∈T , we
ﬁnd the (unique) index i of the chain clause associated with u such thatxi = 0 and xi+1 = 1,
and we deﬁne h(u) to be ai. We similarly deﬁne the images of vertices in B. To see that h is
a homomorphism, assume for the sake of contradiction that an edge {u,v} of G is assigned
to a non-edge of H. Let a and b be the arcs to which u and v are assigned, respectively, and
let x0,...,x t and y0,...,y t′ be the variables associated with u and v, respectively. Then for
some i and j, we have that xi = 0,x i+1 = 1 and yj = 0,y j+1 = 1, from our deﬁnition of
mapping the vertices of G to V (H), we have that a = ai and b = bj. Assume without loss
of generality that ai and bj intersect on the semi-circle from N to S going in the clockwise
direction. Find the smallest j′ such thatbj′ intersectsai on the semi-circle from N toS going
