     1	# arXiv 1308.1068 — deletion list-homomorphism to a skew-sum target
     2	
     3	> **Status:** the generator passes every local G1–G9(c) gate, but the required
     4	> four-vendor oracle run is **not complete**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key
     5	> limit exceeded” on every bare, structural-hint, and placebo call. The
     6	> script-written error transcripts are retained; they are not hardness evidence.
     7	
     8	| profile | value |
     9	|---|---|
    10	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
    11	| Native domain | combinatorics |
    12	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    13	| Computational core | graph |
    14	| Certificate | integer tuple (a sorted deletion-set array) |
    15	| Intuition | decomposition — isolate the one obstructing list-pair block |
    16	| Domain essentiality | native |
    17	| Reduction | none |
    18	
    19	## Problem and trust model
    20	
    21	The solver receives a loopless graph `G`, the target path `H` with edges
    22	`0-1`, `0-3`, and `3-2`, a singleton allowed-image list for every vertex, and a
    23	budget `k`. It must name exactly `k` vertices whose deletion makes the forced
    24	map from `G-W` to `H` a list-respecting graph homomorphism. Requiring exactly
    25	`k` loses no at-most-`k` witness because extra vertices may always be deleted.
    26	
    27	This is the native DL-Hom(`H`) problem defined in Section 2 of Chitnis, Egri,
    28	and Marx, [“List H-Coloring a Graph by Removing Few Vertices”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.1068).
    29	Here `H` is the special sum of two one-edge graphs. Section 3.4 calls an edge
    30	between the `B1` and `T2` list classes a *bad edge*. With the singleton lists
    31	used here, the `1-2` edges are precisely those bad edges; all `0-1`, `0-3`, and
    32	`2-3` blocks already map to target edges.
    33	
    34	Generation composes the bad-edge block from odd paths. On a path with `2d+1`
    35	vertices, the alternating internal side is the unique vertex cover of size
    36	`d`. The generator samples paired paths and random labels, records those
    37	alternating vertices, and only then adds safe edges. No emitted instance is
    38	solved to obtain its answer. Verification checks shape and bounds, then checks
    39	every remaining bad edge by exact integer membership; it never reads
    40	`inst["answer"]` and accepts every valid sorted deletion set.
    41	
    42	## Why Track B, not Track A
    43	
    44	Theorem 1.1 proves fixed-parameter tractability in `k` and `|H|` for
    45	skew-decomposable targets. Section 3.4 supplies the relevant bounded-depth
    46	bad-edge branching, while the Introduction identifies the singleton-target
    47	base case as Vertex Cover. These algorithmic results expressly rule out using
    48	the paper’s worst-case NP-hardness remark as evidence that this inverse
    49	distribution is Track A-hard.
    50	
    51	An efficient algorithm exists here: scan list pairs, run Hopcroft–Karp on the
    52	bipartite obstruction graph, and obtain a minimum cover by Kőnig’s alternating
    53	reachability construction. Its general complexity is `O(E sqrt(V))` time and
    54	`O(E+V)` space. At the shipping preset the measured explicit-block reference
    55	run scanned 4,201,248 edges, recorded 12,607,977 operations, and took 1.256 s;
    56	it solves 8/8 as expected.
    57	
    58	The compact route uses the paper’s special-sum decomposition. The three safe
    59	edge blocks can be discarded symbolically; the remaining symmetric adjacency
    60	rows are disjoint odd paths, so one alternates inward from their endpoints.
    61	That route visits at most 236 obstruction vertices/components. The benchmark
    62	tests whether a no-tool solver finds this compression, not whether DL-Hom is
    63	computationally hard.
    64	
    65	## Worked demo
    66	
    67	The complete `demo`, `seed=0` statement is hand-scale:
